description:
memory.rs:489-511 — get_changes flattens the change log into created/updated/destroyed Vec<Id> by linearly searching with Vec::contains on every iteration:

  for entry in &relevant {
      for id in &entry.created {
          if !destroyed.contains(id) && !created.contains(id) {
              created.push(id.clone());
          }
      }
      for id in &entry.updated {
          if !destroyed.contains(id) && !created.contains(id) && !updated.contains(id) {
              updated.push(id.clone());
          }
      }
      for id in &entry.destroyed {
          created.retain(|c| c != id);
          updated.retain(|u| u != id);
          if !destroyed.contains(id) {
              destroyed.push(id.clone());
          }
      }
  }

This is the textbook 'Vec used as set' pattern. Three issues:
1. Vec::contains is O(n) — each contains() call walks the whole Vec. With M relevant entries each touching N ids, this is O(M·N · (|created|+|updated|+|destroyed|)) — quadratic in the total change count.
2. .retain() is O(n) per call. The 'destroyed.contains' check is preceded by two retains; each is another full sweep.
3. The idiomatic Rust shape uses HashSet for membership tests:

   let mut created_set: HashSet<&Id> = HashSet::new();
   let mut updated_set: HashSet<&Id> = HashSet::new();
   let mut destroyed_set: HashSet<&Id> = HashSet::new();
   // …populate using HashSet::insert/contains
   // …then collect to Vec for the result if order matters

The 'created.retain(|c| c != id)' shape also has a subtle bug: the .retain() inside the loop mutates created.len(), so subsequent created.contains() calls in the same iteration see the post-retain length. That is actually correct for the semantic intent here (a destroyed id is removed from the created bucket before being inserted into destroyed), but it is fragile and makes the algorithm hard to reason about.

The canonical jmap-mail-server has the same loop structure at memory.rs:413-435 — so per workspace AGENTS.md, fix the canonical first.

This is a 'reference impl teaching' concern more than a correctness concern. In production a real backend with a database does this with a query. But contributors reading MemoryBackend as a study guide may carry the Vec-as-set pattern back to production code where it costs more.
